  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand when the greedy algorithm (which chooses the largest possible coins first) will give us the optimal solution for any given amount \\( N \\). This happens when \\( N \\) can be represented as a sum of multiples of 1, 10, and 25 in such a way that no smaller sum exists. In other words, \\( N \\) should not be able to be written as a sum of smaller values of 1, 10, and 25 in fewer coins.\n\nLet's consider the possible values that can be represented using these coins. The smallest value we can represent is 1, which is clearly achievable with one 1-cent coin. The next value is 10, which is achievable with one 10-cent coin. The value 25 is achievable with one 25-cent coin. However, we can't use fewer coins to represent 25 since it's the largest single coin we have.\n\nNow, let's consider the next values. For \\( N = 11 \\), we can't use fewer coins than the greedy algorithm (11 = 1 + 10). For \\( N = 12 \\), we can't use fewer coins than the greedy algorithm (12 = 2 * 10 + 2). For \\( N = 13 \\), we can't use fewer coins than the greedy algorithm (13 = 1 + 2 * 10 + 1). We can see a pattern here: for \\( N = 10k + 1 \\) to \\( 10k + 9 \\), the greedy algorithm will give the optimal solution because we can't use fewer coins than the greedy algorithm.\n\nThe pattern continues until we reach \\( N = 1000 \\). The greedy algorithm will give the optimal solution for \\( N = 10k + 1 \\) to \\( 10k + 9 \\) for each \\( k \\) from 1 to 100. This means we have 100 values of \\( N \\) for which the greedy algorithm will give the optimal solution.\n\nTherefore, the number of values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is \\boxed{100}.",
